iance of the errors is constant across all levels of the factors.\n4. Normality: The residuals should be approximately normally distributed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nThe Plackett–Burman design is applicable when:\n- The researcher has a large number of factors to evaluate.\n- The primary goal is to screen for significant factors rather than to optimize responses.\n- Resources (time, materials) are limited, necessitating a reduced number of experimental runs.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of a Plackett–Burman design typically involves:\n1. Conducting the experiments according to the design layout.\n2. Collecting response data.\n3. Performing regression analysis to estimate the main effects.\n4. Evaluating the significance of the factors using statistical tests (e.g., t-tests or ANOVA).\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Efficiently screens a large number of factors with a minimal number of runs.\n- Simple to implement and analyze.\n- Provides a clear indication of which factors are most influential on the response.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- Cannot estimate interaction effects between factors, which may be critical in some experimental contexts.\n- The design is less effective if the underlying relationships are not linear.\n- Limited to a fixed number of factors and runs, which may not suit all experimental needs.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nAlternative designs include full factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, and response surface methodology (RSM) for optimization after screening. Each of these methods has its own strengths and weaknesses depending on the specific experimental goals.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing a Plackett–Burman design, researchers should ensure that:\n- The factors selected for screening are relevant and manageable.\n- The experimental conditions are controlled to minimize variability.\n- Sufficient replication